-- title:
--   Signed ray law for a finite-order Hecke character on principal ideals
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a number field and let $\xi \colon (\mathbb{A}_K)^\times \to \mathbb{C}^\times$ be a group homomorphism on the units of the adele ring of $\mathcal{O}_K$ in $K$. Assume: $\xi$ is an idele class character, i.e. $\xi$ is trivial on every principal idele $\mathrm{Units.map}$ of the structure map $K \to \mathbb{A}_K$ applied to $u \in K^\times$; $\xi$ is of finite order; $\mathfrak{f}$ is a non-zero ideal of $\mathcal{O}_K$; and $\xi$ admits $\mathfrak{f}$ as a modulus, meaning that $\xi(u) = 1$ for every idelic unit $u$ whose archimedean component is $1$ and whose finite component at each $v$ satisfies $v(u_v) = 1$ and $v(u_v - 1) \le \exp(-m_v)$, where $m_v$ is the multiplicity of $v$ in the factorisation of $\mathfrak{f}$. Let finally $\alpha \in \mathcal{O}_K$ be non-zero with $\alpha - 1 \in \mathfrak{f}$. The conclusion evaluates `raySymbol` for the function $v \mapsto \xi(\varpi_v)$, where $\varpi_v$ is the idele with archimedean component $1$, a chosen uniformizer at the finite place $v$ and $1$ at all other finite places: the finitely supported product $\prod_v \xi(\varpi_v)^{\mathrm{count}_v}$ taken over the fractional ideal attached to the principal ideal $(\alpha)$ equals $\prod_{\tau} \xi_\tau(-1)$, the product over the real embeddings $\tau \colon K \to \mathbb{R}$ with $\tau(\alpha) < 0$ of the local component of $\xi$ at the infinite place determined by $\tau$, i.e. of $\xi$ composed with the embedding of the units of that completion as ideles supported at that place, evaluated at $-1$.
--
--   This is the signed ray-class law for a finite-order Hecke character: the character of ideals defined on prime ideals by $\mathfrak{p}_v \mapsto \xi(\varpi_v)$ takes, on a principal ideal generated by $\alpha \equiv 1 \pmod{\mathfrak{f}}$, the value prescribed by the archimedean signs of $\xi$. It is used in the comparison of a finite-order Hecke character with the determinant of an induced representation in the Langlands–Tunnell step, and in the non-vanishing statements for partial Euler products of the associated Tate zeta integrals.
